# What belongs in the repository is the source, the plans and the build recipe.
# Everything below is either produced by `make`, downloaded, or someone's own
# recordings — with one deliberate exception, noted at the bottom.

# --- built or vendored engines ---------------------------------------------
# `make linux` builds these; `make windows` / `make windows-ffmpeg` fetch them.
binaries/linux/*
binaries/windows/*.exe
binaries/windows/*.dll
binaries/windows/cuda/
!binaries/*/PROVENANCE.md
!binaries/*/FFMPEG-LICENSE.txt

# --- build trees ------------------------------------------------------------
# whisper.cpp checkout, cmake output, the throwaway torch venv for conversions.
.build/

# --- assembled releases -----------------------------------------------------
release_linux/
release_windows/

# --- the program's own output ----------------------------------------------
# Converted audio, transcripts, review state, job queue, settings.
# All of it is reproducible from the source recordings.
work/*

# --- source recordings ------------------------------------------------------
# Other people's audio and video: large, and not always ours to redistribute.
audios/*
videos/*
!audios/.gitkeep
!videos/.gitkeep

# --- helper programs --------------------------------------------------------
# Side tooling that is not part of the shipped program: the downloader, its
# virtualenv and everything it fetches.
helper_programs/

# --- python ----------------------------------------------------------------
.venv/
**/.venv/
__pycache__/
*.pyc

# --- editors and desktops ---------------------------------------------------
.DS_Store
*.swp
.idea/
.vscode/

# --- kept on purpose --------------------------------------------------------
# The corrections collected while reviewing are the one thing here that cannot
# be regenerated: each is a human decision made against the audio, and together
# they are the training set for a future fine-tune. Keep them.
!work/dataset/
!work/dataset/**

# The glossary is hand-written and small, and it changes what the engine hears,
# so it is part of the project rather than output.
!work/glossary.txt
